.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_alloc_entry \- allocate an entry from the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_mempool_alloc_entry(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a pointer to a free entry from the pool. When the pool is empty
and the fallback is on, the function allocates from the heap instead. The
contents of the entry are uninitialized.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Memory pool to allocate from
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the allocated entry on success
Returns NULL when the pool is empty and the fallback is off
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
The memory that comes back is uninitialized
The pointer that comes back is aligned for any object type, the same
guarantee that malloc() makes, so any object that you can store in heap
memory can go into a pool entry
The caller must free the entry with ccol_mempool_free_entry()

On a thread-safe pool this draws from a small cache of the calling
thread and reaches the shared free list only when that cache needs a
refill. A pool too small to keep a cache (fewer than 8 elements) takes
the lock of the pool on every call instead, and so does a thread that
the pool has no cache left to give: one lock for each call, as on a pool
without a cache. A refill, and every call that must go past the cache,
costs one lock. See ccol_mempool_create(3) for how to size a pool; that
page also asks you to measure a candidate configuration rather than
assume one.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers do not contend on the common path.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_calloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_free_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
